/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  Component: Lactic acid (2-hydroxypropanoic acid), crystal (the enantiopure acid) (C3H6O3).  MW 90.078 g/mol (the engine's atomic weights).

  A NON-VOLATILE crystalline organic solid: never enters the vapour (K = 0),
  no Antoine block.  Built on the citric-acid pattern (components/citricAcid.dat); the C372 campaign had left
  this acid without a component, and C373 builds it from the formation data of Miller & Smith-Magowan (1990),
  with Domalski (1972) as the second reading.

  WHAT THIS RECORD CARRIES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT.

    carries   the crystal formation enthalpy dHf = -694.0 +/- 1.0 kJ/mol
              (Miller & Smith-Magowan, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 19 (1990) 1049, Table 5, p. 1056, row L-Lactic, dfH (their ref. b: Cox & Pilcher 1970; read from a page image));
              NO density: the one value in hand (Perry's, Table 2-2) is for the liquid d,l acid, a different phase;
              the heat of solution of the pair file parameters/solution/lacticAcid-water.dat.
    does NOT  a standard entropy s_298 and any heat capacity: no work in hand prints them for this crystal
              as a record value (Miller prints S of the crystal only for succinic and fumaric acid, and it
              enters the ions' entropies through the third-law route of its Table 2, not this record).  An
              energy balance that reaches the crystal's or the solution's SENSIBLE heat therefore refuses by
              name, which is the honest behaviour (CLAUDE.md rule 5).

    ALSO BUILT, in their own homes: the lactate family (HLac, Lac; chemistry/*-formation); pK 3.862 (Nims & Smith 1936, Table II, p. 148).
              Dissolved, this crystal is the molecular master of the family (the hypothetical non-dissociated
              acid); a case that needs the dissociation declares that master.
              Second reading: Domalski, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 1 (1972) 221, Section 6.11, p. 235: D-lactic acid (c) dHf -165.88 kcal/mol x 4.184 = -694.04 kJ/mol (Section 5.5, p. 225), the same crystal value; read from a page image.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

name        lacticAcid;
formula     C3H6O3;
MW          90.078;            // kg/kmol

role        nonvolatile;               // crystalline organic acid; never to the vapour (K = 0)
dissociation 1;                 // molecular (the ions are species/, see the header)

standardThermochemistry
{
    dHf_298   -694000;        // J/mol  crystalline [-694.0 +/- 1.0 kJ/mol, Miller & Smith-Magowan 1990]
    referenceState     pureSolid;
}

provenance
{
    dHf_298
    {
        origin        literature;
        method        "evaluated enthalpy of formation of the crystal (-694.0 +/- 1.0 kJ/mol); second reading: Domalski, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 1 (1972) 221, Section 6.11, p. 235: D-lactic acid (c) dHf -165.88 kcal/mol x 4.184 = -694.04 kJ/mol (Section 5.5, p. 225), the same crystal value; read from a page image";
        citation      "Miller & Smith-Magowan, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 19 (1990) 1049, Table 5, p. 1056, row L-Lactic, dfH (their ref. b: Cox & Pilcher 1970; read from a page image)";
    }
}
